<<<
+ place for summering in, for they are not again spoken of; here are shows that seemed is wrong. 3. To my &c. seeming. From wherever
===
+ *place for summering in, for they* are *not again spoken of*; here *are* shows that *seemed* is wrong.

**3.** *To my &c. seeming.* *From wherever
>>>

<<<
he may start, he is sure to bring us out very presenily into the road along which, to his seeming, our primitive ancestors must have travelled.
===
he may start, he is sure to bring us out very presently into the road along which,* to his seeming, *our primitive ancestors must have travelled.*
>>>

<<<
To my &c. seeming has been good English in its time; its modern representative is to my &c. thinking, & to his seeming will pass only in archaic writing.
===
To my &c. seeming has been good English in its time; its modern representative is *to my &c. thinking,* & *to his seeming* will pass only in archaic writing.
>>>

<<<
unarchaic ‘ primitive ancestors °’.
===
unarchaic 'primitive ancestors'.
>>>

<<<
4. As seem(s) to be the case. How can the Labour Ministry acquire proper authority if it has powers so limited as seem to be the case?
===
**4.** *As seem(s) to be the case.* *How can the Labour Ministry acquire proper authority if it has powers so limited as seem to be the case?*
>>>

<<<
As seem to be the case is always impossible, because the relative pronoun as, for which see AS 5, never represents an expressed plural noun (such as powers here),
===
*As seem to be the case* is always impossible, because the relative pronoun *as*, for which see ^^AS^^ 5, never represents an expressed plural noun (such as *powers* here),
>>>

<<<
As seems to be the case is, then, the only right form of the phrase; but even that will not do here, because it involves the doubling of two parts by as, that of relative adverb,
===
*As seems to be the case* is, then, the only right form of the phrase; but even that will not do here, because it involves the doubling of two parts by *as*, that of relative adverb,
>>>

<<<
indispensable after the preceding so, & that of the relative pronoun required by the otherwise subjectless verb seems.
===
indispensable after the preceding *so*, & that of the relative pronoun required by the otherwise subjectless verb *seems*.
>>>

<<<
Waat has happened is this. The writer wanted to say if it has powers so limited as its powers seem to be.
===
What has happened is this. The writer wanted to say *if it has powers so limited as its powers seem to be.*
>>>

<<<
He shied at the repetition of powers, & felt about for as seems to be the case as a substitute, though he forgot to alter seem to seems. But,
===
He shied at the repetition of *powers*, & felt about for *as seems to be the case* as a substitute, though he forgot to alter *seem* to *seems*. But,
>>>

<<<
But,

since so makes the relative pronoun

as impossible, the true solution was

to let the as be a relative adverb,

writing if its powers are so limited

as they seem to be.
===
But, since *so* makes the relative pronoun *as* impossible, the true solution was to let the *as* be a relative adverb, writing *if its powers are so limited as they seem to be.*
>>>

<<<
seemly. For the adverb, see -LILY.
===
**seemly.** For the adverb, see ^^-LILY^^.
>>>

<<<
seer has double pronunciation &

+ meaning: & ser

sé’er beholder, $3 prophet &c. segregate.
===
**seer** has double pronunciation &

+ meaning: sē'er beholder, & sēr prophet &c.

**segregate.**
>>>

<<<
Verb -at, adj. -it or -at 5 see PARTICIPLES 5 C. Seidlitz. Pronounce séd-. seigneur &c. Spellings recognized
===
Verb *-āt*, adj. *-ĭt* or *-at*; see ^^PARTICIPLES^^ 5 C.

**Seidlitz.** Pronounce sĕd-.

**seigneur &c.** Spellings recognized
>>>

<<<
in the OED as current: seigneur, seignior; seigneuress; seigneury, seigneurie, seign(i)orage, seign(i)ory; seigneurial, seign(i)or(i)al. The pronunciation in all begins with san followed by the y sound.
===
in the OED as current: seigneur, seignior; seigneuress; seigneury, seigneurie, seign(i)orage, seign(i)ory; seigneurial, seign(i)or(i)al. The pronunciation in all begins with sān followed by the y sound.
>>>

<<<
Differences in meaning or use between alternative forms (as seigneur & seignior, seigneury & seigniorage) cannot be detailed here, but exist & are sometimes of importance.
===
Differences in meaning or use between alternative forms (as *seigneur* & *seignior*, *seigneury* & *seigniorage*) cannot be detailed here, but exist & are sometimes of importance.
>>>

<<<
seise, seisin. Pronounce séz, sé’zin.
===
**seise, seisin.** Pronounce sēz, sē'zĭn.
>>>

<<<
The words are sometimes but less often spelt -ze, -zin, & belong etymologically to the ordinary verb seize;
===
The words are sometimes but less often spelt *-ze*, *-zin*, & belong etymologically to the ordinary verb *seize*;
>>>

<<<
but in the legal phrases to $. a person of, i.e. put him in possession of, & to be -ed of, i.e. to possess, the -sspelling is usual.
===
but in the legal phrases *to s. a person of*, i. e. put him in possession of, & *to be -ed of*, i. e. to possess, the -s-spelling is usual.
>>>

<<<
seize makes -zable; see MUTE E. For the spelling seise, see above.
===
**seize** makes *-zable*; see ^^MUTE E^^. For the spelling *seise*, see above.
>>>

<<<
seldom can be compared with -er, -est; see -ER & -EST 3.
===
**seldom** can be compared with *-er*, *-est*; see ^^-ER^^ & ^^-EST^^ 3.
>>>

<<<
select, vb, makes -tor; see -OR.
===
**select,** vb, makes *-tor*; see ^^-OR^^.
>>>

<<<
Seleucid. Pl. -ids or -idae.
===
**Seleucid.** Pl. *-ids* or *-idae*.
>>>

<<<
self. As both self & wife were fond of seeing life, we decided that .. ./
===
**self.** *As both* self & wife *were fond of seeing life, we decided that . . ./*
>>>

<<<
He ruined himself & family by his continued experiments.
===
*He ruined* himself & family *by his continued experiments.*
>>>

<<<
Correct the first to both I & my wife, & the second to himself & his family.
===
Correct the first to *both I & my wife*, & the second to *himself & his family*.
>>>

<<<
Such uses of s. are said by the OED to be ‘ jocular or colloquial ’ extensions of a ‘ commercial’ idiom; &, unless the jocular intent is unmistakable, they are best avoided.
===
Such uses of *s.* are said by the OED to be 'jocular or colloquial' extensions of a 'commercial' idiom; &, unless the jocular intent is unmistakable, they are best avoided.
>>>

<<<
self-. Self- compounds are sometimes used when the self- adds nothing to the meaning.
===
**self-.** *Self-* compounds are sometimes used when the *self-* adds nothing to the meaning.
>>>

<<<
Agricultural depression & the rural exodus had made village life self-despondent & anaemic./Hence it is self-evident that economic changes in the agricultural system must greatly affect the general well-being.
===
*Agricultural depression & the rural exodus had made village life* self-despondent *& anaemic.*/*Hence it is* self-evident *that economic changes in the agricultural system must greatly affect the general well-being.*
>>>

<<<
There is perhaps never any difference of meaning between despondent & self-despondent. Self-evident, on the other hand,
===
There is perhaps never any difference of meaning between *despondent* & *self-despondent.* *Self-evident,* on the other hand,
>>>
